From a1b57e1d5c747b3f90e626b51db2ebf5dab1cac3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fix <fix@local>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 12:53:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] panfrost: drain GPU work before putting BO back into cache

When panfrost_bo_unreference() returns a BO to the cache, the BO is
immediately marked evictable via PANFROST_MADV_DONTNEED so the kernel
shrinker can reclaim it under memory pressure.

This races with in-flight GPU jobs in two ways:

  1. The job's WRITE fence can signal on the CPU side before the GPU's
     LS caches finish draining stale data for the BO. The next
     fragment/tiler submission that re-imports the BO (either from the
     cache or after the kernel has purged and re-mapped it) then hits
     DATA_INVALID_FAULT because the LSU still sees the old mappings.

  2. The kernel shrinker may reclaim and unmap the BO while the GPU
     is still mid-load, surfacing the same fault. The kernel's
     gpu_usecount check catches jobs that have already attached a fence
     via panfrost_job_pin(), but it does not see jobs that have been
     armed but not yet started by the job scheduler.

Panfrost job slots use START_FLUSH_CLEAN_INVALIDATE/END_FLUSH_CLEAN_
INVALIDATE to flush their own LSU state on entry and exit, so waiting
for the WRITE fence is sufficient to guarantee the BO is no longer
referenced by the GPU. Other drivers (i915, msm, crocus, panthor) all
wait before allowing eviction; panfrost was the odd one out.

Call panfrost_bo_wait() before pan_kmod_bo_make_evictable() so the
common case (gpu_access == 0, no work outstanding) is a cheap lock +
check with no ioctl. The new code path acquires locks in the order
bo_cache.lock -> bo_access_lock, matching panfrost_bo_cache_fetch()
and avoiding any new deadlock risk.

This eliminates the DATA_INVALID_FAULT + shrinker race observed with
Firefox + WebRender on Mali-G57 (Bifrost) under memory pressure, where
the cache_put/madvise path could hand a still-active BO to the kernel
shrinker.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_bo.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_bo.c b/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_bo.c
index d98ea00..1e0e6ba 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_bo.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_bo.c
@@ -277,6 +277,24 @@ panfrost_bo_cache_put(struct panfrost_bo *bo)
    if (bo->flags & PAN_BO_SHARED || dev->debug & PAN_DBG_NO_CACHE)
       return false;
 
+   /* Drain any GPU work still referencing this BO before handing it
+    * back to the cache. Without this, a recent job's WRITE fence may have
+    * already signalled on the CPU side while the GPU's LS caches still
+    * hold stale data for this BO. Marking the BO evictable (and therefore
+    * purgeable by the kernel shrinker) under those conditions lets the
+    * kernel unmap the page tables while the LSU is still mid-load, which
+    * surfaces as DATA_INVALID_FAULT jobs from the next fragment/tiler
+    * submission and pollutes the address space until the next shrinker
+    * sweep clears it.
+    *
+    * panfrost_bo_wait() takes bo_access_lock internally and reuses the
+    * cached gpu_access state established by the submit path, so the
+    * common case (gpu_access == 0, no work outstanding) is a cheap
+    * lock + check with no ioctl.
+    */
+   if (!panfrost_bo_wait(bo, INT64_MAX, true))
+      return false;
+
    /* Must be first */
    pthread_mutex_lock(&dev->bo_cache.lock);
 
